WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.140
When I see G.R.R. Martin’s White Walkers
depicted in the Game of Thrones series, I

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.080
have to tell you, I couldn’t help but think
they have a lot in common with the draugr

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.410
, especially in appearance.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.350
You obviously know of the draugr 
through your numerous studies of Ancient Nordic

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.610
culture, or while playing hours of Skyrim.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:23.880
But in the far-off chance you haven’t done
either of those things, let me explain; Draugrs

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:30.670
are ancient, Nordic undead, humanoid monsters
who rise from their graves to kill, wreak

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.940
havoc, and generally disrupt the living.?

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:40.260
What can draugr say about vikings, ancient
burial ceremonies, and cooking dinner…um…naked?

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:44.960
To me, what’s so fascinating about these
creatures is how they can teach us so much

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.149
about the lives of the people who lived thousands
of years ago and that some of the strongest

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:54.000
Nordic warriors were actually women.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.990
We know of draugrs from the Sagas of the Icelanders,
stories that relate the early history and

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:12.150
people of Iceland from the late ninth century
to the end of the “Viking age.”

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:18.330
Yes, I am using air quotes because the term
Viking is not a national or ethnic identity,

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:21.480
but a profession—the act of raiding.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.000
Two kinds of undead creatures emerge from
these sagas: the Haugbúi,

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.300
I don't know, that thing.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:32.900
who pretty much

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.590
just hung out in their burial sites, leaving
people alone, and the Draugr, who left their

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.240
graves to harm the living.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.060
There were very specific reasons why a newly-buried
person would transform into either one of

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.060
these monsters.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.740
Common, and seemingly random, reasons for
a person to become either type of undead monster

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.560
included: practicing black magic, being executed
as capital punishment, dying an accidental

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.600
death (try avoiding that one), or being buried
outside of a churchyard.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.979
Once reanimated the draugr are said to have
been more active at night and in the winter.

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:09.509
Sometimes the appearance of the draugr was
foretold by a mist or temporary darkness…again,

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:10.509
this feels familiar....

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:14.260
Draugr are scary!

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.590
They’ll kill you and your livestock, and
engage in one of their favorite pastimes,

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.120
“riding roofs.”

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.290
Which from what I can gather pretty much just
meant stomping around on the roof at night

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.080
making noise so people can’t get any sleep.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.640
But let’s not forget, they’ll also kill
you.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.300
These monsters are often described as possessing
enormous strength.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:41.940
Like the Ancient Egyptians, the old Nordic
people believed that you could literally take

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.440
what you were buried with to the next plane
of existence.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.720
A huge piece of evidence for this is the grave
goods that have been found at burial sites.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.980
The Nordic people also practiced the “corpse-door”
custom.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.860
It was believed that the dead could only return
the way they left the home.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.580
So to keep the dead from returning, a wall
or portion of a house would be removed to

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.010
carry the corpse outside for burial.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.720
Then, the corpse-door would be quickly repaired.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.290
This prevented the undead from making any
surprise visits.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.980
All of these cultural practices are reflected
in the Sagas of the Icelanders through encounters

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.470
with draugrs.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:20.910
Many of these monsters are a test for the
story’s hero or heroine, and often reflect

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.410
power struggles between different kinship
groups or even within families.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:30.790
Perhaps the most famous draugr story pops
up in Grettis saga when the hero Grettir defeats

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.730
Glamr, a particularly aggressive draugr in
an epic wrestling match.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.560
Grettir ends up sending Glamr to a permanent
death by decapitating him then burning his

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.900
body and burying the ashes.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.450
Here we find the only way to successfully
“re-kill” a draugr.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:51.551
It’s a method that pops up in other sagas:
The only way to immobilize a draugr is to

00:03:51.551 --> 00:03:54.810
chop off the head, burn the body, and scatter
the ashes.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:04:01.520
This is actually one thing Skyrim got right—fire
is an EXCELLENT weapon against draugr...AND

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.260
White Walkers…just saying.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:07.090
In “The Saga of the People of Eyri,” a
wall is built around a new burial site to

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.360
prevent a particularly dangerous draugr from
returning.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:15.730
The wall was so high that quote “no one
could see over it except a bird in flight.”

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.930
They literally build a wall to try and stop
the undead.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:22.330
How is this not an influence for Game of Thrones?

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:25.500
But not all draugr are violent monsters.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.699
One such example is Thorgunna.

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:31.800
She was so offended by the poor hospitality
offered to the men carrying her corpse to

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.879
her burial that she comes back from the dead
to make dinner for them, naked, before being

00:04:36.879 --> 00:04:39.419
buried (restfully this time) at a church.

00:04:39.419 --> 00:04:40.639
The best part?

00:04:40.639 --> 00:04:42.050
They all eat the food.

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:43.869
Would you eat zombie food?

00:04:43.869 --> 00:04:49.020
Now we have to talk about my favorite Icelandic
story, The Saga of King Heidrek the Wise.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:54.300
It’s amazing in so many ways, but mostly
because of the shieldmaiden Hervör —who

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.310
absolutely should become your new favorite
literary heroine.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:03.479
Hervör, who is “as strong as a man” and
trained in bow, shield, and sword, decides

00:05:03.479 --> 00:05:08.159
she wants to retrieve a dwarven magical sword
buried with her father and uncles.

00:05:08.159 --> 00:05:12.639
She puts on male clothes, changes her name
to herself Hervard, and joins up with some

00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:13.639
vikings.

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:18.830
And because she’s a total boss, she even
becomes captain of her own band of vikings.

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:22.669
Hervör travels many miles to reach her ancestral
burial mound.

00:05:22.669 --> 00:05:27.479
Her father tries to scare her away, but Hervör
is having none of that it and she manages

00:05:27.479 --> 00:05:30.479
to convince him to give her the sword.

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:35.409
After a little more livin’ it up as a viking,
she grows tired of it all, hangs up gear and

00:05:35.409 --> 00:05:38.000
retires, eventually getting married and having
children.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:44.160
A huge part of Ancient Nordic and particularly,
Icelandic sagas, is the inclusion of undead

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.499
monsters threatening the lives, and sanity,
of the living.

00:05:47.499 --> 00:05:53.499
Their proximity to death and their religious
beliefs made burial a sacred but often imperfect

00:05:53.499 --> 00:05:58.969
practice and that their kinship with ancestors
was so important it could literally last beyond

00:05:58.969 --> 00:05:59.969
the grave.

00:05:59.969 --> 00:06:03.200
To me what's so fascinating about these creatures

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:06.480
is how they can teach us so much about the lives of the people

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.680
who lived thousands of years ago

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.160
Thanks for watching Monstrum.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.240
Do you have a favorite monster or myth you’d
like see on the show?

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:15.869
Let me know in the comments!

00:06:15.869 --> 00:06:19.089
And don’t forget to subscribe!

